  "slug": "protein-model-93eb",
  "summary": "Recent preprints test whether task-adapted protein language models outperform established homology and structure search tools.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is adapting sequence-based representations to a defined prediction task rather than using generic embeddings directly. MicroDUET attaches phylogeny and function adapter heads to a compact genomic model for pathogen surveillance. SMSTPred uses protein language model embeddings to predict SAM-methyltransferase substrate types, and CarmnaDD predicts nitrogenase activity from sequence-derived dual descriptors. S-PLM-TM adds structure awareness to a protein language model to support sequence-only remote homology search. Know Thy Neighbour offers a counterpoint: benchmarking embedding-based retrieval against MMseqs2 and Foldseek, it reports that anisotropy substantially impairs performance. deeprok addresses a related but distinct problem, prokaryotic gene prediction, using a neural network with overlap-aware decoding and reporting fewer errors than Prodigal and GeneMarkS-2. The novelty lies in explicit task conditioning and in direct comparison against established sequence and structure search baselines.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
